Z||000000||pmd68gto||12-04-2001||09:41 PM||||i have a 84 fiero i just bought with check engine light on, read codes, replace ox sensor, tps, fan cooling switch, then just code 45, replaced ecm, would not start, found ground g202 wire broken, fixed end, since then battery would not charge in car, installed new battery next morning dead again<BR>took g202 lose again installed fully charged battery, tried junk yard ecm, started, ran ok<BR>but still code 45, next day dead battery.  help, i think it must be a short somewhere, where do you start looking? <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||12-04-2001||10:30 PM||||Hey GTO!<BR>Welcome to the forum!<BR>Start with a volt/ohm meter, nothing fancy, just a cheep radio shack job will do...<BR>Disconnect the positive battery cable and check the amps flowing in the circuit with everything off...<BR>If you have 1 to 5 amps flowing, disconnect the battery terminal at the starter, carefull, the solenoid bakelite cap may be brittle and crack or break...<BR>If this stops the current flow, then the starter could be bad, or the power taps at the starter could be draining the battery...<BR>If it is the starter, get a rebuild...<BR>If it is the power taps, then you need to determine which one, and where it goes...<BR>One goes to the alternator, the other goes to S200...<BR>If it is not any of these, there is a power tap at the battery, it goes to S503, the fuel injection...<BR>Beyond this, you need schematics to troubleshoot the problem...<BR>The Haynes manuals are still available and provide wiring diagrams...<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GTDude||12-04-2001||10:33 PM||||GM Tech here.  First try unplugging the alternator overnight.  Check that trunk light isn't staying on.  If that fails...disconnect negative battery cable and put a digital volt meter in between the cable and the battery.  If it reads 12volts then you have a major drain.  If this is the case....pull one fuse at a time until the voltage drops.  When this happens you have found the circuit in which the problem lies.  You will need a wiring diagram to find out what runs on that circuit.  OH!  Be sure to hold the door button closed so you won't get a false reading.  Hope this helps.||||reg||10
Z||000003||pmd68gto||12-05-2001||12:30 AM||||thanks for the replies, i think i may have just found it, the positive battery cable was<BR>skent where it goes beside the block, do you think this could have also caused the code 45<BR>(low voltage, rich fuel) ? thanks<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||10
